Model Design & Architectural Evolution:
As required, I built and trained a custom CNN entirely from scratch, without
using any external datasets and pre-trained weights and APIs.
* Iteration 1 (Baseline): Started with a simple 2-block CNN (3x3 convolutions
  and MaxPool) just to make sure the data pipeline and model saving mechanism
  worked.
* Iteration 2 (Scaling up): Increased to a 4-block CNN. I replaced the standard
  Flatten layer with AdaptiveAvgPool2d so the dimensions wouldn't break, and
  added BatchNorm2d and standard Dropout to help with gradient stability and
  overfitting.
* Iteration 3-4 (Dealing with Augmentations): Added heavy data augmentations.
  This made the training loss very noisy with high variance, and the model
  struggled to learn.
* Iteration 5 (Final - Double Convolutions): I changed each block to a Double
  Convolution structure (Conv->BN->ReLU->Conv->BN->ReLU->Pool). This gives the
  network a larger receptive field before pooling, helping it learn actual
  physical shapes instead of just memorizing background colors.

Manipulations Tried, Insertion Strategy & Impact on Performance:
To stop the model from "shortcut learning" (like guessing it's a duck just
because the background is blue), I added data augmentations directly into the
PyTorch DataLoader during training. Here is how each one affected the model:

1. RandomResizedCrop (Zooming and Cropping):
   * Strategy: Randomly zooms and crops the image.
   * Impact: At first, it lowered my validation accuracy because the bird
     wasn't always perfectly centered. However, it forced the model to learn
     specific body parts (like wings or beaks), which ended up improving the
     accuracy on the stress-test images.
2. RandomHorizontalFlip (Orientation):
   * Strategy: Applied with a 50% probability.
   * Impact: This barely hurt the training loss and effectively taught the
     model that the direction the bird is facing doesn't change its class.
3. ColorJitter (Brightness, Contrast, Hue):
   * Strategy: Randomly changes the colors and lighting.
   * Impact: This caused the biggest drop in accuracy at the beginning because
     it messed up the background colors the model was relying on. But
     eventually, it forced the optimizer to ignore color shortcuts and look at
     the actual structure of the bird.

Overall Performance Resolution:
Combining all these augmentations made it hard for the model to converge, and
it got stuck around 34% on the robust stress-test data. To fix this, I added
Spatial Dropout inside the conv blocks. Instead of dropping
random pixels, this drops entire feature maps, so the model can't rely on just
one specific visual cue. I also added a StepLR scheduler to lower the learning rate over the 30 epochs and help the model
settle into a local minimum cause i didnt want to miss the minimum if the learning rate is too big.

In the end, the model reached ~68.35% on the clean validation set and ~46.33%
on the stress-test set, showing a good balance between learning the training
data and handling visual noise.